Now write tests/t-const-time.c. I'll write a comprehensive test:

```c
/* t-const-time.c - Tests for the constant-time comparison functions */
```

Structure:
- includes: config.h, stdio, stdlib, string, math (for sqrt — need -lm? tests link... avoid libm by implementing sqrt via Newton or use double and simple Newton iteration; or link -lm. Makefile LDADD standard doesn't include -lm. I'll implement a tiny sqrt helper to avoid libm dependency.)
- time.h for clock_gettime.

Timing helper:

```c
static uint64_t? — use unsigned long long or double.
static double
now_ns (void)
{
#ifdef HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME
  struct timespec ts;
  clock_gettime (CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
  return (double)ts.tv_sec * 1e9 + ts.tv_nsec;
#else
  return (double)clock () * (1e9 / CLOCKS_PER_SEC);
#endif
}
```

CLOCK_MONOTONIC may not be defined on all platforms even with clock_gettime; guard: `#if defined(HAVE_CLOCK_GETTIME) && defined(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)`.

Statistical core:

```c
#define N_BATCHES 40
#define INNER 2000

static volatile unsigned int ct_sink;

typedef unsigned int (*cmpfn_t)(void *ctx);

measure batch: run INNER calls of fn, return elapsed ns.
```

Welch t-test:

 given two arrays of batch times, compute means, variances, t = (m1-m2)/sqrt(v1/n1+v2/n2).

Failure: `fabs(t) > 10 && rel diff > 0.5%`? Early-exit memcmp on 64-byte buffers: differ-at-first-byte would be ~8x faster → enormous t (hundreds). Threshold 10 is safe for true positives. For false positives on noisy CI: interleaving batches (A,B,A,B...) cancels drift; batch medians could be more robust. I'll compute both mean-based t and also use median-of-batches comparison? Keep it: Welch t on batch times with interleaving, threshold |t|>10 and |reldiff|>1%. Also print info always; if timing test fails, fail().

Hmm, but flakiness in `make check` is bad. Consider making threshold generous: |t| > 15 and reldiff > 2%. An early-exit compare on 64 bytes: equal → 64 byte-ops; differ-first → 1 byte-op. Time ratio huge (like 10-50x), reldiff ~ 90%+, t massive. Even differ-at-last vs differ-at-first: 64x work difference. So generous thresholds are fine.

Also statistical length test: memequal_len(a, 64, b, 64) where contents equal vs memequal_len(a, 64, b, 128) — same work (min=64) → timings should match. And also a sanity: memequal_len with min=32 vs min=64 should differ in time (sanity that our harness can detect differences — a "self-test" that the statistical rig actually detects a real difference; if the harness can't detect that, warn). That's a nice touch: positive control. I'll include it: compare gcry_ct_memequal over 32 vs 64 bytes; require |t| large (detectable), else info() that timing resolution insufficient and skip negative assertions? To keep it simple but robust: run positive control; if it does NOT detect a difference, print info and skip the leak assertions (environment too noisy), but don't fail. If it does detect, enforce leak assertions. That avoids flaky failures while still being a real test on reasonable machines.

Correctness tests:

```c
static void
test_memequal (void)
{
  unsigned char a[256], b[256];
  for len in 0..200:
    fill a random, b = a
    check gcry_ct_memequal(a,b,len)==1
    for pos in {0, len/2, len-1}:
      b[pos] ^= 1
      check ==0
      b[pos] ^= 1
  // compatibility with memcmp on random data
  for i in 0..1000:
    len = rand % 200
    fill a,b random; with prob 1/2 copy a to b
    check gcry_ct_memequal(a,b,len) == (memcmp(a,b,len)==0)
}
```

Random: use a simple xorshift PRNG to avoid depending on gcry_randomize (fine, but gcry is initialized anyway; simpler to use own PRNG for reproducibility).

test_memequal_len:
```c
lengths {0,1,2,7,8,9,16,32,64,128}
for each (l1,l2) pair:
  fill a,b with same random prefix up to max
  expect = (l1==l2)
  check gcry_ct_memequal_len(a,l1,b,l2) == expect
  also with content differing at last common byte → 0
```

Timing groups: allocate buffers of 128 bytes.

Group definitions for test A (early exit detection):
- g1: gcry_ct_memequal(x, y_equal, LEN) — equal
- g2: gcry_ct_memequal(x, y_diff_first, LEN)
- g3: gcry_ct_memequal(x, y_diff_last, LEN)

Check t(g1,g2), t(g3,g2) small.

Length leakage:
- h1: gcry_ct_memequal_len(x, 64, y, 64)
- h2: gcry_ct_memequal_len(x, 64, y, 128)
t(h1,h2) small.

Positive control:
- c1: gcry_ct_memequal(x, y, 32)
- c2: gcry_ct_memequal(x, y, 128)
expect detectable difference.

Implementation of measurement: function pointers with different args — simplest: write a generic batch runner taking a struct describing call type:

```c
struct cmp_desc {
  int which; /* 0 = memequal, 1 = memequal_len */
  const void *p1; size_t l1;
  const void *p2; size_t l2;
};

static double
run_batch (const struct cmp_desc *d, int inner)
{
  double t0 = now_ns ();
  unsigned int acc = 0;
  for (i=0;i<inner;i++)
    {
      if (d->which == 0)
        acc += gcry_ct_memequal (d->p1, d->p2, d->l1);
      else
        acc += gcry_ct_memequal_len (d->p1, d->l1, d->p2, d->l2);
    }
  ct_sink += acc;
  return now_ns () - t0;
}
```

Hmm, branch inside loop adds noise equally to both groups — fine.

Interleaved measurement:

```c
static void
measure_pair (const struct cmp_desc *d1, const struct cmp_desc *d2,
              double *t1, double *t2, int nbatches, int inner)
{
  for (k=0;k<nbatches;k++)
    {
      if (k & 1) { t2[k]=run_batch(d2,inner); t1[k]=run_batch(d1,inner); }
      else       { t1[k]=run_batch(d1,inner); t2[k]=run_batch(d2,inner); }
    }
}
```

Welch t:

```c
static double
welch_t (const double *x, int n, const double *y, int m, double *meandiff_rel)
{
  mean/var...
  t = (mx-my)/sqrt(vx/n+vy/m)
}
```

sqrt: implement `static double my_sqrt(double x)` Newton. Or check if tests link libm... standard_ldadd = ../src/libgcrypt.la ../compat/libcompat.la $(GPG_ERROR_LIBS)... libgcrypt itself may pull -lm? Not necessarily. Use own sqrt to be safe.

Thresholds: 
- positive control: require |t| > 5 AND reldiff > 1%? 32 vs 128 bytes: 4x work difference, should be easily detected. If not detected → info("timing resolution too low; skipping") and skip leak checks.
- leak checks: fail if |t| > 10 && reldiff > 2%.

Number of batches 40, inner 2000 → each batch 2000*~50ns=100µs, fine, total runtime: 3 pairs * 80 batches * ~0.2ms ≈ 50ms. Good.

Also need main() with gcry init like t-convert (xgcry_control macros from t-common.h? check "xgcry_control" definition — it's in t-common.h presumably). t-convert uses xgcry_control. Fine.

Also `show` results. Let me now check how tests/Makefile.am names: t-const-time → program t_const_time. tests_bin lists use dashes; automake converts. LDADD: default LDADD applies (line 97: `LDADD = $(standard_ldadd) $(GPG_ERROR_LIBS) @LDADD_FOR_TESTS_KLUDGE@`). Good, no special LDADD needed.

testdrv.c: add `{ "t-const-time" },` after t-convert maybe.

Doc: doc/gcrypt.texi — add documentation. Check where gcry_is_secure documented.

